# Update ai-standards

Two modes, one procedure file.

**Cold start.** If you are reading this file from an ai-standards checkout because the target
project has no deployment yet — or one older than this skill — this file is your procedure:
clone the repository named by the user (or the one you are reading it from), make sure
`uv run ai-sync` works in the checkout (`uv sync`), then run the mode the request calls for.
Once the deployment exists, the deployed copy of this skill activates in the project on the
bare phrase.

**Routing.** No `ai.project.toml` in the target project → Install mode. A deployment exists →
Upgrade mode; when the deployed skill is missing or older than this file, run `render` +
`sync-templates` before the digest so the project holds the current procedure and adapters.

## Install Mode

1. **Acquire the source.** Clone the repository named by the user into a maintenance location
   (ask when the convention is unknown), run `uv sync` in the checkout, and confirm
   `uv run ai-sync --help` works.
2. **Survey the project.** Existing `AGENTS.md` (rendered or hand-written — if hand-written,
   ask how to merge before overwriting anything); stacks in play against the registry's
   `[stacks]`; docs layout and language; ConPort or Basic Memory already configured; CI;
   tracker; which agent environments the team uses (`codex`, `claude`, `kilo`, `cursor`).
3. **Propose the feature set.** From `[features]` in `registry.toml`, one line per feature —
   what it changes for this project, a recommendation for or against based on the survey, and
   a link to its usage guide under `docs/`. Nothing is enabled by default.
4. **Apply on confirmation.** Run `uv run ai-sync init-project --project-root <project>`, then
   fill `ai.project.toml`: the confirmed `features` (related ones adjacent), `stacks`,
   `tooling.agents` from the survey, and `[metadata]`: `project_name`, `standards_source`
   (the repository URL — later updates depend on it), `tracker_url` when the project names a
   tracker. Then `render` + `sync-templates` + `check` + `doctor`.
5. **Report.** What was surveyed, what was enabled, the checks that ran, and that the bare
   phrase now works in the project. Committing the manifest and rendered file follows the
   project's own git workflow.

## Upgrade Mode

1. **Establish the deployed version.** Read `ai_standards_version` from `ai.project.toml`;
   fall back to the `Generated by ai-standards` header of the rendered `AGENTS.md`.
2. **Establish the latest release.** The source is the `standards_source` entry under
   `[metadata]` when present, otherwise the checkout known to this session; ask when neither
   is known. Bring the checkout up to date (`git pull`), read its release version from
   `pyproject.toml` under `tool.ai-standards`, and say so when the checkout is ahead of its
   latest tag.
3. **Report an already-current deployment and stop** when the deployed version equals the
   latest release.
4. **Bootstrap when needed.** If the deployed skill is missing or older than this file, run
   `render` + `sync-templates` first, so the project holds the current procedure and adapters.
5. **Refresh enabled features silently.** `render` + `sync-templates`. Text updates to
   already-enabled features need no confirmation.
6. **Announce what is new and not yet enabled.** Features whose `[feature_meta].since` is
   newer than the deployed version **and that are absent from the manifest `features` list**:
   one line each — what changes for this project, a link to its usage guide, and a
   recommendation against the project's `stacks`. Cover notable skill and workflow additions
   from the CHANGELOG sections between the two versions as well.
7. **Ask once.** Apply only what the user confirms: add the confirmed features to `features`
   (related ones adjacent — `module-contract-gate` right after `structured-artifacts`), set
   `ai_standards_version` to the latest release, re-run `render` + `sync-templates`.
8. **Verify and report.** `check` + `doctor`; report the old and new versions, what was
   refreshed, what was enabled, and that the agent should be restarted to load the new
   `AGENTS.md`.

## Never

- Never enable a feature without the user's explicit confirmation in this run.
- Never push to any remote as part of an install or update.
- Never invent features, versions, or usage-guide links; read them from the checkout.
- Never treat a stale pin as silent: when `ai_standards_version` disagrees with the source in
  use, say so before rendering.
- Never overwrite a hand-written `AGENTS.md` without asking how to merge it.

## Report

State the mode, the deployed and latest versions, what was surveyed or refreshed, what was
enabled with the user's confirmation, the checks that ran, and the next step for the user —
restarting the agent and reviewing the new `AGENTS.md`.
